          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 space-y-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Can a standard cron expression run every 30 seconds?</h3>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
                Standard POSIX/Unix crontab does not support seconds; its minimum resolution is one minute (<code class="text-white font-mono">1m</code>). However, frameworks like Quartz (Java), Spring, or modern runner daemons support 6-field cron expressions with a seconds column. For Unix crontab, running twice a minute is achieved by running two commands with a <code class="text-white font-mono">sleep 30</code> offset.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 space-y-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Is Sunday represented as 0 or 7 in cron?</h3>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
                Most modern cron parsers (including Linux Vixie cron, cronie, and CronGen) accept both <code class="text-white font-mono">0</code> and <code class="text-white font-mono">7</code> as Sunday, where 1 is Monday and 6 is Saturday. However, POSIX strictly specifies <code class="text-white font-mono">0 = Sunday</code>. To ensure 100% portability across old systems, use <code class="text-white font-mono">0</code> or the named abbreviation <code class="text-white font-mono">SUN</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-5 space-y-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Why did my cron expression not run on the server?</h3>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
                The most frequent reasons are: missing newline at the end of the crontab file, timezone mismatch between UTC and local time, lack of file execution permissions (<code class="text-white font-mono">chmod +x</code>), or unreferenced shell paths in the environment.
              </p>
